<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/colour-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson deepens one third of the foundational <strong>Value, Colour,
    Composition</strong> lesson into a theory of its own. There you met value and hue as
    drawing levers; here the levers acquire their physics and their psychology —
    wavelengths, three cone types, opponent channels — and the practical machinery that
    follows from them: mixing systems, contrast effects, and the wheel as a relationship
    menu. The sibling lessons <strong>Line, Shape and Value</strong> and
    <strong>Perspective and Depth</strong> carry the other levers; everything here is
    about colour alone.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe any colour in three dimensions — hue, value, saturation — and explain why value is the load-bearing one;</li>
    <li>trace colour from physics to perception: wavelengths, trichromacy, the opponent process, colour constancy;</li>
    <li>keep additive (screens) and subtractive (pigments) mixing separate, and predict each system's complements;</li>
    <li>use simultaneous contrast, afterimages and the Bezold effect as working tools rather than surprises;</li>
    <li>choose colour schemes as controlled relationships — with dominance and accessibility built in.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The page's spine is one finding: colour is a relationship, not a property. Everything
    else follows from taking that seriously.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Colour theory is <strong>the physics of light meeting the psychology of perception</strong> —
    the physics lesson's wavelengths plus the perception lesson's relativity. Its core finding
    (colour is a relationship, not a property) is why palettes work and random colour samplers
    don't. Three facts organise everything in this page: every colour has three dimensions and
    they are not equally important; pigments and light mix by different arithmetic; and colour
    perception is context-dependent to a degree that startles every beginner. Master those
    three and the colour wheel stops being a poster on the wall and becomes a design tool.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dimensions, mixing modes, interaction, schemes" widget="equationStep" config={colour2Cfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels stack into one argument. Panel 1
    ranks the three dimensions so value carries the load; panel 2 separates the two
    mixing arithmetics so screen results stop surprising paint results; panel 3 delivers
    Albers's core fact — colour is relative, judged against its field; panel 4 turns the
    wheel into a menu of relationships rather than a law book. Read them in order once,
    then revisit panel 3: it is the one that changes how you look.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the page's promise is that the wheel becomes a design tool
    once three facts are in place — three dimensions, two mixing arithmetics, one
    relativity. First: the dimensions themselves, ranked by how much work each does.
  </p>

  <h2>Three dimensions — and why value is the load-bearing one</h2>
  <p>
    Every colour is a point in a three-dimensional space. <strong>Hue</strong>: which colour
    — the wheel position, from red through green to violet. <strong>Value</strong>: lightness
    versus darkness, independent of hue (a lemon and an aubergine can share a hue family and
    differ almost entirely in value). <strong>Saturation</strong> (Munsell says
    <em>chroma</em>): intensity versus grey — the difference between a fire alarm and a
    faded brick. The Munsell system formalises this as hue/value/chroma coordinates, and
    designers live in its descendants (HSL in software is a rough cousin). The hierarchy
    that practice teaches: <strong>value first, then temperature, then saturation, then
    hue</strong>. A painting with correct values and wrong hues still reads (a Monet in
    greyscale still organises); a painting with wrong values fails no matter how refined
    the hues. Early vision extracts lightness structure before hue registers — the reason
    every colour-weakness accommodation and every "convert to greyscale" design check is
    really a statement about value. Tint (hue + white), shade (hue + black), tone (hue +
    grey) are just value travels along one hue.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> every colour has three dimensions, and practice ranks them —
    value first, then temperature, then saturation, then hue. Next: where colour comes
    from at all, and why the eye's two-layer design explains the phenomena painters see.
  </p>

  <h2>The physics: wavelengths, three cones, four opponents</h2>
  <p>
    Light is a spectrum; colour is the visual system's summary of it. Newton's prism
    experiments (1666–72) showed white light decomposing and, crucially, recombining —
    the wheel was born from physics, not aesthetics. Human colour vision rests on three
    cone types (peaked near yellow-green, green and blue-violet; Young proposed
    trichromacy in 1802, Helmholtz refined it), which is why three primaries suffice to
    match almost any colour experience — and why colour TV needs exactly three channels.
    But the second layer explains the phenomena artists see: Hering's <strong>opponent
    process</strong> (1892) — vision encodes red-versus-green and blue-versus-yellow
    differences, which is why there is no "reddish green", why staring at red produces a
    cyan afterimage (successive contrast: the fatigued opponent channel rebalances), and
    why complementary schemes feel inevitable rather than clever. The perceptual endpoint:
    <strong>colour constancy</strong> — the brain discounts the illuminant so a white
    shirt looks white in tungsten or daylight — is the same machinery that makes colour
    <em>relative</em> on the canvas, as Albers would demonstrate.
  </p>

  <h2>Two mixing systems: additive and subtractive</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Additive</strong> (light): combine wavelengths and the eye sums them — red +
    green light = yellow; all three = white. Screens run on RGB. <strong>Subtractive</strong>
    (pigment): each pigment absorbs part of the spectrum and the residue reaches the eye —
    mix more, subtract more; all together ≈ muddy brown. Printing runs on CMY(K), the
    efficient subtractive primaries (magenta + yellow make red; cyan + yellow make green).
    The trap lives in the difference: the traditional painter's wheel (red-yellow-blue,
    descending from the 18th-century print tradition) gives different complements than the
    physical ones — on a printer's wheel, red's partner is <strong>cyan</strong>, not green.
    Practical consequences for painters: the vivid modern paints (cadmiums, phthalos,
    quinacridones) behave closer to the subtractive ideal, so <em>blue + orange</em> and
    <em>red + green</em> are the complements that actually neutralise each other on the
    palette; and every designer who has watched a screen-perfect RGB palette collapse in
    CMYK print has met gamut — the set of colours a device can physically produce — the
    hard way. Worked example of the perceptual arithmetic: a violet shadow. Skylight is
    blue; warm light is orange; an object's shadow side is lit by the sky — so a cool
    (blue-violet) shadow against a warm-lit object is not a stylistic fantasy but roughly
    what the physics delivers, which is why Monet's violet shadows on snow (<em>The
    Magpie</em>, 1869 — scandalous to the Salon, obvious to a photographer) look true
    the moment you see them outdoors.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> red, yellow and blue are the "real" primary colours, fixed by physics. Primaries are chosen per system: screens mix red, green and blue light additively; printers mix cyan, magenta and yellow subtractively; the painter's red-yellow-blue wheel is an eighteenth-century convention whose complements agree with neither. There is no master set — only the set that fits the medium you are mixing in.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two mixing systems, two wheels, and a trap between them —
    primaries are chosen per medium, and the complements follow the medium, not the
    poster. Next: the demonstrations that make colour's relativity impossible to unsee.
  </p>

  <h2>Colour is a relationship: Albers and the relativity demonstrations</h2>
  <p>
    Josef Albers spent a decade (Yale, published 1963 as <em>Interaction of Color</em>)
    building the exercises that prove colour's relativity with paper, and they remain the
    permanent curriculum. The canonical demonstration: one grey square on a blue field
    appears warmer (pushed toward its complement, orange); the same grey on an orange
    field appears cooler. Extend the principle and the working toolkit falls out.
    <strong>Simultaneous contrast</strong> affects value too (identical greys differ on
    black and white fields) — so never judge a colour in isolation, only in situ.
    <strong>Afterimages</strong> (successive contrast) reveal the opponent channels and
    explain why a small complement accent "vibrates" next to its partner — the electric
    red-on-green of van Gogh's <em>Night Café</em> is opponent-process physiology used
    deliberately. The <strong>Bezold effect</strong> — one colour changed everywhere
    shifts the whole pattern's read — is why palette changes feel global. And
    <strong>metamerism</strong> (two colours matching under one light, diverging under
    another) is why galleries specify lighting and why clothes that matched in the shop
    fight in daylight. The meta-lesson is Albers's own: colour is "the most relative
    medium in art" — you do not choose colours, you choose <em>conditions</em>, and the
    viewer's eye finishes the job.
  </p>

  <h2>Schemes: the wheel as a relationship menu</h2>
  <p>
    The colour-wheel schemes are shorthand for controlled relationships.
    <strong>Complementary</strong> (opposites: blue/orange, red/green, yellow/violet) —
    maximum contrast for accents and focal points; <em>mixed</em>, they give the most
    natural neutrals and shadows (a touch of orange into a blue shadow keeps it alive;
    black tends to deaden chroma). <strong>Analogous</strong> (wheel neighbours) —
    instant harmony, at the cost of needing value or temperature contrast to separate
    elements. <strong>Split-complementary</strong> (a hue plus the two neighbours of its
    opposite) — complementary energy with less shout and more nuance.
    <strong>Triadic</strong> (three evenly spaced hues) — vivid balance that wants one
    hue dominant. <strong>Monochrome</strong> (one hue, value and saturation travel) —
    mood by value alone. Two disciplines apply to all schemes. First, dominance:
    the 60-30-10 habit (or any lopsided ratio) beats equality — a scheme is a hierarchy,
    not a democracy. Second, the limited palette: three pigments plus white (the Zorn
    palette — yellow ochre, cadmium red, ivory black, titanium white — painted whole
    portraits with astonishing range) forces mixing discipline, and mixtures within a
    small palette harmonise automatically because they share ancestry; accidental
    harmony is a property of constraints, which every design discipline in this atlas
    rediscovers independently.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> shadows are made with black paint. Black lowers the value but deadens the chroma, leaving a grey hole where the physics suggests skylight — the light's cool complement — fills the shadow side of a warm-lit object. A touch of the complement darkens while keeping the shadow alive, which is why the Salon found Monet's violet snow shadows scandalous and photographers find them obvious.</p>

  <h2>Warm and cool, culture and access</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Temperature</strong> is the perceptual axis that cuts across hue: warm
    (red-yellow) advances, cool (blue-green) recedes — the psychological companion of a
    physical fact, <strong>atmospheric perspective</strong>: air scatters short (blue)
    wavelengths (Rayleigh scattering, the same reason the sky is blue), so distant
    mountains literally are lighter and bluer; painters from Leonardo's blue Alps to
    every landscape course since encode the scattering curve. Warm/cool interplay also
    organises abstract work (balance a cool field with a warm accent and the eye has
    both a home and a destination). <strong>Cultural encoding is real and must be
    checked, not assumed</strong>: white is mourning in much of East and South Asia,
    red carries luck and celebration in China (and danger in Western finance), purple
    priced royalty for a century because the dye was priced like silver. Working
    globally means checking the chromatic map before shipping. And <strong>access</strong>:
    red-green colour vision deficiency affects roughly 8% of men and 0.5% of women, so
    hue alone must never carry meaning — pair every hue distinction with a value or
    shape distinction, the same value-first rule wearing a design-systems badge.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> colour-blind people see the world in greyscale. Total colour blindness is exceedingly rare; the common red-green deficiencies shift or confuse specific hue pairs while the rest of colour vision works normally — which is exactly why the design rule is to add a value or shape redundancy to every hue distinction, not to design without colour.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> temperature organises space, cultures encode meanings that
    must be checked rather than assumed, and access demands value redundancy behind every
    hue distinction. Next: a critical history of the wheels themselves — knowing the
    lineage sharpens the instrument.
  </p>

  <h2>Wheels and systems: a short critical history</h2>
  <p>
    The colour wheel you drew in school is one instrument among several, and knowing
    the lineage sharpens its use. Newton's 1704 wheel was physics (spectral hues bent
    into a circle, with purple as the mixture the spectrum lacks); Goethe's 1810
    <em>Theory of Colours</em> was psychology and physiology — afterimages, contrast,
    the felt temperature of hues — scorned by physicists and invaluable to painters;
    Munsell (1905) built the three-dimensional hue-value-chroma tree that industrial
    colour matching still uses; and the Bauhaus turned theory into pedagogy, with
    Johannes Itten's seven contrasts as a curriculum and Albers's relativity
    exercises as its corrective. The live debate between the two Bauhaus lines is
    instructive: Itten taught colour as universal law (complementary harmony as
    prescription); Albers taught colour as context-dependent experience (exercises
    over rules). Modern practice blends them — the schemes give you a starting
    hypothesis, the interaction effects decide in situ. One practical residue of
    the history: the traditional R-Y-B artist's wheel, the printer's C-M-Y wheel
    and the screen's R-G-B wheel genuinely disagree about complements — the
    "opposites" that neutralise best in paint (blue-orange, red-green) are
    physical mixing facts, and the wheel you use should match the medium you
    mix in.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two identical grey squares sit on a saturated blue field and an orange field. What happens, and why?</li>
    <li>Mixing blue and yellow paint gives green. What does mixing blue and yellow light give, and why the difference?</li>
    <li>Why should a design remain readable in greyscale — and what does that rule have to do with value?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Each grey reads shifted toward the field's complement —
    warm on blue, cool on orange — because the visual system calibrates against its
    surroundings (simultaneous contrast): colour is a relationship, which is why matching
    colours from memory is useless. (2) A whitish grey: light mixes additively, so blue
    and yellow wavelengths — near-complementary in additive mixing — sum toward white,
    while pigments mix subtractively by absorbing wavelengths; the two wheels are
    different wheels. (3) Because the visual system extracts value structure before hue
    registers, and roughly 8% of men have red-green colour vision deficiency: never
    encode meaning in hue alone — give every hue distinction a value or shape distinction
    to lean on.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every colour has three dimensions — hue, value, saturation — and value is the load-bearing one.</li>
    <li>Colour vision is two-layer: three cone types (trichromacy) plus opponent channels (no reddish green; afterimages).</li>
    <li>Screens mix light additively; pigments mix subtractively — primaries and complements differ by medium.</li>
    <li>Colour is relative: simultaneous contrast, afterimages and the Bezold effect are tools, not errors.</li>
    <li>Shadows take the light's complement; black lowers value but deadens chroma.</li>
    <li>Schemes are relationship menus — run with dominance (the 60-30-10 habit) and limited palettes for automatic harmony.</li>
    <li>Never encode meaning in hue alone: value redundancy is free accessibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Line, Shape and Value</strong> builds the value-first structure this page
    keeps deferring to, <strong>Perspective and Depth</strong> develops atmospheric
    perspective — the physics behind warm advancing and cool receding — into a full
    system, and <strong>Drawing from Observation</strong> trains the eye that judges
    every colour in situ rather than in isolation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The economics of pigment.</strong> Colour history is material history:
    ultramarine was ground from lapis lazuli mined in Afghanistan and priced above gold
    — Renaissance contracts specified how much the patron would pay for it, and Vermeer's
    blue-heavy palette was quietly ruinous. Synthetic ultramarine (1828) collapsed the
    price; cobalt, cadmium and the aniline dyes (1850s–60s, from coal tar) arrived in
    time for the Impressionists to paint sunlight in tubes — the movement's plein-air
    colour range is partially a chemistry story. Every palette is an inventory of what
    industry could make that year.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Colour spaces and their quirks.</strong> Digital colour space is a
    three-dimensional coordinate system whose axes do not map cleanly to perception:
    equal RGB steps are visibly unequal, HSL's "lightness" makes saturated yellow read
    far lighter than saturated blue at the same number, and perceptually uniform
    spaces (CIELAB, and the newer OKLCH) exist precisely because the naive spaces lie.
    Gamut mapping — squeezing a screen image into print's smaller subtractive gamut —
    is where theory meets invoices. The professional habit: soft-proof against the
    destination space, and treat out-of-gamut hues as design decisions, not surprises.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The linguistic frontier.</strong> How much does language shape colour
    perception? The classic Berlin–Kay sequence (colour terms enter languages in a
    fixed order: black/white, red, then green/yellow, blue...) suggests universals;
    the Himba studies (faster discrimination among greens than blues, with matching
    vocabulary) suggest lexical effects on speed of discrimination — the current,
    careful synthesis: perception is universal, <em>attention and memory</em> for
    colour differences are partly trained by the categories your language hands you.
    For an artist the lesson is practical: expand your colour vocabulary deliberately
    (name the exact green — sap, viridian, olive, sage) and you will see more of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Individual variation is larger than practice
    assumes — tetrachromacy (a fourth cone type, mostly in women, in a fraction of the
    population) may grant genuine extra discrimination; the physics of why some
    complementary pairs "vibrate" while others harmonise is only partly reduced to
    opponent channels; and the hard problem — why 700 nm <em>feels like</em> red —
    remains where it has always been. Colour theory, unusually among the visual
    fundamentals, is a live science: Albers's exercises are fifty years old and still
    the cheapest laboratory in the building.
  </p>
